 ## Overview
-Octree Volume Compression is a lossless (or semi-lossless) technique that reduces the memory footprint of structured volume data by converting it into an octree structure and consolidating voxels with zero variance [S2, S3][S4, unofficial]. It achieves compression factors of 3 to 5 for typical simulation data by exploiting spatial coherence, where large uniform regions are represented by single nodes [S2][S4, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [SVO Node Encoding](2705-svo-node-encoding.md) by focusing on the initial construction-phase compression of the raw grid into the hierarchy, often utilizing iso-range clamping to discard irrelevant data values before or during tree construction [S2][S4, unofficial].
