+The method builds upon the [Implicit Octree Storage](939-implicit-octree-storage.md) structure [G]. The resulting octree includes an embedded min/max tree to support efficient ray traversal and isosurface extraction [S2, S3][S4, unofficial]. The total size reported in benchmarks includes this overhead [S2][S4, unofficial]. Exact field layouts, sizes, and bit-packing schemes are Not given in the sources.
 
 **Steps**
-1. **Variance Consolidation (Lossless):** Traverse the volume grid and identify regions where all voxels have identical values (zero variance) [S1, S2, S3][S4, unofficial]. Replace these regions with a single octree node representing the uniform value [S1, S2, S3][S4, unofficial]. This is the primary mechanism for lossless compression [S1, S2, S3][S4, unofficial].
-2. **Iso-Range Clamping (Optional Lossless):** Define a target isovalue range $[min_{iso}, max_{iso}]$ of interest [S2, S3][S4, unofficial]. For every voxel value $v$ in the input grid: if $v < min_{iso}$, set $v \leftarrow min_{iso}$; if $v > max_{iso}$, set $v \leftarrow max_{iso}$ [S2][S4, unofficial]. This clamping increases the likelihood of zero-variance regions outside the range of interest, allowing them to be consolidated into the tree [S2][S4, unofficial]. Values within the range remain unchanged, preserving lossless quality for those isosurfaces [S2][S4, unofficial].
