+2. **Iso-Range Clamping (Optional Lossless):** Define a target isovalue range $[min_{iso}, max_{iso}]$ of interest [S2, S3][S4, unofficial]. For every voxel value $v$ in the input grid, apply clamping:
+   $$v' = \begin{cases} min_{iso} & \text{if } v < min_{iso} \\ max_{iso} & \text{if } v > max_{iso} \\ v & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}$$
+   where $v'$ is the clamped value [S2][S4, unofficial]. This clamping increases the likelihood of zero-variance regions outside the range of interest, allowing them to be consolidated into the tree [S2][S4, unofficial]. Values within the range remain unchanged, preserving lossless quality for those isosurfaces [S2][S4, unofficial].
 3. **Octree Construction:** Build the octree hierarchy from the (optionally clamped) volume data by consolidating zero-variance regions into nodes [S2][S4, unofficial].
 4. **Lossy Consolidation (Optional):** If lossy compression is acceptable, apply a non-zero variance threshold during consolidation to aggressively merge inter-voxel variance and further quantize isovalues [S2, S3][S4, unofficial].
